      },
      "title": "How a Hydrogen Fuel Cell Works",
      "eyebrow": "ENERGY CONVERSION FUNDAMENTALS",
      "subtitle": "Chemical reactions, voltage, current, and a simple power calculation"
    },
    {
      "text": "A hydrogen fuel cell converts the chemical energy of hydrogen and oxygen directly into electrical energy. In a typical proton exchange membrane fuel cell (PEMFC), the main byproducts are water and heat.",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "",
        "fontSizePt": 10.5,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 8,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "1. How the fuel cell works",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "#0F766E",
        "fontSizePt": 16,
        "fontWeight": 700,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 8,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 5,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "numbered",
      "items": [
        "Hydrogen gas ($H_2$) enters the anode, where a platinum catalyst separates each molecule into protons ($H^+$) and electrons ($e^-$).",
        "The proton exchange membrane allows protons ($H^+$) to pass through to the cathode, but it blocks electrons ($e^-$).",
        "The electrons are forced through an external electrical circuit. Their movement produces direct-current electricity for a load, such as an electric motor.",
        "Oxygen ($O_2$), usually supplied from air, reaches the cathode. There, oxygen combines with the returning electrons ($e^-$) and the protons ($H^+$) to form water and release heat."
      ],
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "",
        "fontSizePt": 10.5,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 6,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Electron path: anode \u2192 external circuit/load \u2192 cathode. Proton path: anode \u2192 membrane \u2192 cathode.",
      "type": "callout",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 8,
        "paddingPt": 12,
        "textColor": "#164E63",
        "fontSizePt": 10,
        "fontWeight": 600,
        "accentColor": "#06B6D4",
        "borderColor": "#A5F3FC",
        "marginTopPt": 8,
        "borderWidthPt": 1,
        "marginBottomPt": 10,
        "backgroundColor": "#ECFEFF"
      },
      "title": "Why electricity is produced",
      "eyebrow": "KEY IDEA"
    },
    {
      "text": "2. Chemical reactions",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "#0F766E",
        "fontSizePt": 16,
        "fontWeight": 700,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 7,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 5,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Anode (oxidation):\n$$H_2 \\rightarrow 2H^+ + 2e^-$$\nCathode (reduction):\n$$\\frac{1}{2}O_2 + 2H^+ + 2e^- \\rightarrow H_2O$$\nOverall reaction:\n$$H_2 + \\frac{1}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow H_2O$$",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 6,
        "paddingPt": 10,
        "textColor": "#1F2937",
        "fontSizePt": 11,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "#D5DEE8",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0.5,
        "marginBottomPt": 7,
        "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Under standard conditions (25 \u00b0C and 1 atm), the theoretical reversible cell voltage is approximately 1.23 V. It follows from the Gibbs free-energy relation $$E^\\circ = -\\frac{\\Delta G^\\circ}{nF}$$ using $\\Delta G^\\circ \\approx -237.13\\ \\mathrm{kJ/mol}$, $n = 2$ electrons, and $F \\approx 96{,}485\\ \\mathrm{C/mol}$.",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "",
        "fontSizePt": 10.5,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 6,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Real cells operate below 1.23 V because of activation losses, ohmic resistance, and mass-transport limitations. A loaded PEM fuel cell commonly operates around 0.6\u20130.8 V per cell, depending on conditions and design.",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "",
        "fontSizePt": 10.5,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 6,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "page_break",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "",
        "fontSizePt": 10,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 0,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "3. Calculating electrical power",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "#0F766E",
        "fontSizePt": 16,
        "fontWeight": 700,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 7,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 5,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Stack voltage:\n$$V_{\\mathrm{stack}} = N \\times V_{\\mathrm{cell}}$$\nElectrical power:\n$$P = V_{\\mathrm{stack}} \\times I$$\nCombined form:\n$$P = N \\times V_{\\mathrm{cell}} \\times I$$\nElectrical energy over time:\n$$E = P \\times t$$",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 6,
        "paddingPt": 10,
        "textColor": "#1F2937",
        "fontSizePt": 11,
        "fontWeight": 400,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "#D5DEE8",
        "marginTopPt": 0,
        "borderWidthPt": 0.5,
        "marginBottomPt": 10,
        "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "4. Simple numerical example",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "style": {
        "align": "left",
        "radiusPt": 0,
        "paddingPt": 0,
        "textColor": "#0F766E",
        "fontSizePt": 16,
        "fontWeight": 700,
        "accentColor": "#0F766E",
        "borderColor": "",
        "marginTopPt": 7,
        "borderWidthPt": 0,
        "marginBottomPt": 5,
        "backgroundColor": ""
      }
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Number of cells in series, N",
          "80"
        ],
        [
          "Operating voltage per cell, $V_{\\mathrm{cell}}$",
          "0.70 V"
        ],
        [
          "Operating current, I",
          "40 A"
        ],
